In this study, ten standard benchmark datasets are used to evaluate the classification efficiency of the proposed ensemble45. These benchmark imbalanced datasets, their characteristics, and sources / records are summarized in Table 1. These imbalanced datasets are representative of a wide range of machine learning applications, ranging from biomolecules to distinguishing between nasal and oral sounds, from clinical settings to automobile industry, and from characteristics of astroparticles to email spams. Moreover, these datasets are robust and versatile by offering a spectrum of features (d), observations (n), uneven distribution of observations in minority and majority classes, and imbalance ratios (IR). All these datasets are publicly available in online repositories, where their sources are mentioned in Table 1 (last column).Table 1
Summary of ten benchmark imbalanced datasets, their characteristics and sources.
