First, the approach of under-sampling the majority class (U) was used so that half of the observations were discarded from the majority class while maintaining the observations of minority class. Nevertheless, the discarded observations may contain critical information, thus negatively affecting the predictive performance of base classifiers. Second, the approach of oversampling the minority class (O) was used, where observations from the minority class were regenerated (i.e., doubled). Although over-sampling (i.e., doubling) the minority class sustained all the information, the regeneration of extra training observations was time consuming and computationally extensive. Third, a hybrid sampling technique (i.e., a combination of both over-sampling and under-sampling) was employed to generate a more balanced training dataset47. Specifically, the over-sampling with-replacement to double the minority class observations (i.e.,\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
